<%# locals: (rotation:, plan:, roster:, vault:, eligible:, required:, max_slots:, server_address:, identities:) %>
<%#
  THE EVICTION CONSOLE.

  IT LEADS THE PAGE, and that is the point of the ordering rather than a layout
  preference. The four authority panels under it are REFERENCE; this is the act
  the operator opened the page to perform.

  TWO STEPS, AND THE SPLIT IS THE DESIGN:
    ARM      — record the proposed set. Builds NOTHING. The operator reads the
               exact new signer set with no clock running.
    CO-SIGN  — mint fresh bytes at CLICK time, collect the signatures, broadcast.

  WHY NOT BUILD AT ARM TIME. A built transaction carries a recent blockhash with
  a ~60-90 second life. Building it while he reads would mean the bytes are dead
  before he clicks, which is exactly the defect that left $140 of payouts unsent
  from June to September on the treasury queue. THE DURABLE NONCE IS NOT THE FIX
  and cannot be: a nonce transaction is only recognised when
  `advanceNonceAccount` is instruction 0, and Phantom injects its own Lighthouse
  guard instructions ahead of whatever was built. So the window is real, and the
  answer is to start it when he acts.

  EVERY ADDRESS HERE CARRIES ITS HOLDER WHERE THERE IS ONE. Five full base58
  strings with no hierarchy is what this card used to be, and an operator cannot
  pick a key to remove by comparing forty-four characters by eye. Resolving is
  not asserting: a wallet with no app account renders as visibly unresolved, and
  the full address renders on every row either way.
%>

<%# THE DESTRUCTIVE ROW ACTION, DEFINED ONCE.

    IT MUST NOT WEAR THE AFFIRMATIVE COLOUR. `btn-outline` draws its border from
    `--color-cta`, which in this app is the same green as `btn-primary` — so the
    per-row Evict buttons came out the same colour as the "Arm this eviction"
    call to action directly beneath them. Two buttons, one colour, opposite
    meanings, on the one card where a mis-click removes a signing key.

    Danger INK on a bare surface, not a danger FILL: the fill belongs to the
    submit that commits the plan, and three filled red buttons stacked down the
    card would out-shout it. The classes are written literally here because
    Tailwind scans source TEXT — a name assembled at render time compiles to no
    rule at all and paints nothing. %>
<% evict_button_class = "btn btn-sm shrink-0 border border-danger bg-transparent " \
                        "text-danger-ink hover:bg-danger hover:text-white" %>

<%# THE FIELDS HAD NO STYLING AT ALL, AND THAT WAS INVISIBLE.

    Both controls carried `input input-bordered`, and NEITHER class exists: the
    engine's field primitive is `input-field`, and a colour- or box-shaped class
    the stylesheet does not define paints nothing while reading perfectly in the
    markup. So the slot inputs rendered as bare text on the surface — three
    base58 strings an operator had no way to tell were editable, on the one card
    where typing into them is the entire point.

    The primitive is adopted rather than hand-rolled, with only the density
    retuned: its own padding is sized for a full-width form and these rows carry
    four to a card. Written as literal tokens because Tailwind scans source
    TEXT and a name assembled at render time compiles to nothing — which is the
    same failure this comment is about.

    STILL PRESENT ELSEWHERE: /admin/vault_state and /admin/vault_init carry
    eight more of the phantom pair between them. Out of scope here because they
    are other pages with their own layouts to re-check, not because they are
    correct. %>
<% signer_field_class = "input-field w-full rounded-md px-2 py-1.5 font-mono text-2xs" %>

<%# WHAT ALPINE NEEDS TO PAINT A HOLDER IT HAS NOT SEEN BEFORE.

    The slot inputs are editable and the authorizer selects change under the
    operator's hand, so the identity beside them cannot be server-rendered once
    and left alone. The map is handed over as JSON on a data attribute and read
    in `init()` — the same discipline the factory note below records for every
    other prop, and for the same reason: an interpolated `x-data` expression
    dies silently on one stray quote. %>
<% identity_map = identities.each_with_object({}) do |(address, user), map|
     map[address] = {
       name: user.display_name,
       initials: user.avatar_initials,
       color: user.avatar_color,
       avatar: (user.avatar.attached? ? url_for(user.avatar) : nil),
       kind: (user.web3_solana_address == address ? "Phantom" : "Managed")
     }
   end %>

<section class="card p-4 mb-6 border-danger/30">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-1">Evict a vault signer</h2>
  <p class="text-secondary text-sm mb-4">
    Writes a new <code>VaultState</code> signer set. This is the only real remedy
    for a stolen vault key, and it is irreversible without another rotation.
  </p>

  <%# A CLAIMED ROW IS ANSWERED FIRST, AHEAD OF THE UNREADABLE-VAULT REFUSAL.
      Surfacing it needs no chain read, and the refusal below exists to stop a
      rotation being PLANNED blind — a different act from resolving one already
      sent. The RPC that lost the broadcast's answer is the same RPC this page
      reads, so the two failures arrive together; letting the second hide the
      first is how a stranded eviction became a Rails console. %>
  <% if rotation&.submitted? %>
    <%= render "stranded", rotation: rotation, plan: plan, identities: identities %>

  <% elsif vault.nil? %>
    <p class="text-sm text-warning-ink">
      The vault signer set could not be read, so nothing can be planned against
      it. Refusing to guess.
    </p>

  <% elsif rotation.present? && plan.present? %>
    <%# ── ARMED: SHOW THE EXACT NEW SET BEFORE ANYTHING IS SIGNED ────────── %>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3 mb-4"
         data-eviction-armed
         data-eviction-slug="<%= rotation.slug %>">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted mb-2">
        Armed — nothing has been signed
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="label-upper text-3xs text-muted mb-1">Signer set now</p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5">
            <% Array(plan[:current]).each do |address| %>
              <%= render "armed_wallet", address: address, user: identities[address],
                         tone: (Array(plan[:evicted]).include?(address) ? "text-danger-ink line-through" : "text-heading") %>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="label-upper text-3xs text-muted mb-1">
            Signer set after — <%= pluralize(Array(plan[:proposed]).length, "key") %>
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5">
            <% Array(plan[:proposed]).each do |address| %>
              <%= render "armed_wallet", address: address, user: identities[address],
                         tone: (Array(plan[:added]).include?(address) ? "text-primary" : "text-heading") %>
            <% end %>
            <% (max_slots - Array(plan[:proposed]).length).times do %>
              <li class="text-2xs text-muted">— empty —</li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <% if Array(plan[:evicted]).any? %>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-danger-ink">
          <strong>Evicting <%= pluralize(Array(plan[:evicted]).length, "key") %>:</strong>
          <% Array(plan[:evicted]).each do |address| %>
            <span class="block">
              <span class="font-bold"><%= identities[address]&.display_name || "No linked account" %></span>
              <span class="font-mono text-2xs break-all"><%= address %></span>
            </span>
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% end %>
      <% if Array(plan[:added]).any? %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-primary">
          <strong>Adding:</strong>
          <% Array(plan[:added]).each do |address| %>
            <span class="block">
              <span class="font-bold"><%= identities[address]&.display_name || "No linked account" %></span>
              <span class="font-mono text-2xs break-all"><%= address %></span>
            </span>
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-secondary">
        Signed by <%= pluralize(plan[:required_signatures].to_i, "wallet") %>,
        led by
        <span class="font-mono text-2xs"><%= plan[:lead_signer] %></span><%= plan[:server_leads] ? " (this server)" : " (your wallet — it pays the network fee)" %>.
        Program shape <strong><%= plan[:shape] %></strong>.
      </p>

      <%# THE RULE THAT DECIDES WHO MAY LEAD. Stated where it bites, because it
          is counter-intuitive and it is the reason the server cannot always be
          the one to sign. %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-3xs text-muted">
        Continuity requires <%= plan[:required_signatures] %> of the wallets that
        authorize a rotation to survive it — so
        <strong>a wallet that signs this transaction cannot be evicted by it</strong>.
        That is why the lead is chosen rather than assumed: evicting this
        server’s own key means the server must not sign.
      </p>
    </div>

    <%= render "shared/wallet_desktop_only_notice" %>

    <% if roster.present? %>
      <div class="mb-4" data-cosign-controls>
        <%= render "admin/pending_transactions/signer_roster",
                   roster: roster,
                   required_signatures: plan[:required_signatures].to_i,
                   identities: identities %>

        <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button data-tx-slug="<%= rotation.slug %>"
                  data-rebuild-url="<%= admin_rebuild_authority_rotation_path(rotation.slug) %>"
                  data-broadcast-url="<%= admin_broadcast_authority_rotation_path(rotation.slug) %>"
                  onclick="cosignTransaction(this.dataset.txSlug, 'Signer Eviction', [], this, { rebuildUrl: this.dataset.rebuildUrl, broadcastUrl: this.dataset.broadcastUrl, successNoun: 'signer rotation', backLabel: 'Back to Authorities' })"
                  data-desktop-only-action
                  class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">
            Collect <%= plan[:required_signatures] %> signatures &amp; evict
          </button>

          <%= button_to "Discard", admin_cancel_authority_rotation_path(rotation.slug),
                        method: :post, class: "btn btn-outline btn-sm",
                        form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Discard this armed eviction?" } } %>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-2 text-3xs text-muted">
          The transaction is built when you click, not now — so the ~60-90 second
          blockhash window starts then and you are never racing a clock you did
          not start. If it does expire, click again: it rebuilds from scratch
          and nothing stale is ever re-sent.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

  <% else %>
    <%# ── NOTHING ARMED: THE PLANNER ─────────────────────────────────────── %>
    <%= form_with url: admin_arm_authority_rotation_path, method: :post,
                  data: { turbo: false } do |f| %>
      <%# TWO STEPS, TWO ENCLOSURES, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A REAL DEFECT.

          The slot list ends in a footnote — "N of M slots filled; K signatures
          are required, so the set cannot go below K" — and with only vertical
          rhythm between the groups that sentence sat flush against the WALLETS
          THAT WILL SIGN heading below it. One section's conclusion read as the
          next section's preamble, which on this card means a sentence about the
          SIGNER SET reads as a rule about the SIGNING WALLETS. They are
          different sets with different rules, and confusing them is the exact
          mistake the continuity note further down exists to prevent.

          Boxing each step states the boundary structurally instead of hoping a
          margin carries it. %>
      <div x-data="evictionPlanner()"
           x-init="init($el)"
           data-current-signers="<%= Array(vault[:active_signers]).join(',') %>"
           data-max-slots="<%= max_slots %>"
           data-required="<%= required %>"
           data-identities="<%= identity_map.to_json %>"
           class="space-y-5">

        <section class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3">
          <p class="label-upper text-xs mb-1">Step 1 — the new signer set</p>
          <p class="text-3xs text-muted mb-2">
            Slots are left-packed: turf-vault refuses a gap, so “empty” can only
            ever be a suffix. Evicting compacts the rest up for you.
          </p>

          <template x-for="(slot, i) in slots" :key="i">
            <div class="mb-1.5 rounded-md border border-subtle bg-surface p-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-3xs font-bold uppercase text-muted" x-text="i + 1"></span>
                <input type="text" name="signers[]" x-model="slots[i]"
                       placeholder="empty"
                       class="<%= signer_field_class %>">
                <button type="button" class="<%= evict_button_class %>"
                        x-show="slots[i]" @click="evict(i)"
                        :aria-label="'Evict slot ' + (i + 1)">Evict</button>
              </div>

              <%# WHO HOLDS THIS SLOT. Three siblings with `x-show` rather than
                  one `<template x-if>` per state: a template needs a single root
                  element and silently mounts NOTHING when given more than one,
                  and three plain spans cannot fall into that. %>
              <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 pl-8"
                   x-show="slots[i]" x-cloak>
                <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full
                             bg-cover bg-center text-xs font-bold text-white"
                      x-show="identityFor(slots[i])" x-cloak
                      :style="avatarStyle(slots[i])"
                      x-text="initialsFor(slots[i])"></span>
                <span aria-hidden="true"
                      class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full
                             border border-dashed border-strong text-3xs font-bold text-muted"
                      x-show="!identityFor(slots[i])" x-cloak>?</span>
                <span class="text-xs"
                      :class="identityFor(slots[i]) ? 'font-bold text-heading' : 'text-muted'"
                      x-text="labelFor(slots[i])"></span>
                <span class="rounded px-1 py-px text-3xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
                      :class="identityFor(slots[i]) ? 'bg-surface-alt' : 'border border-dashed border-strong'"
                      x-text="kindFor(slots[i])"></span>

                <%# THE KEY IN FULL, ON THE ONE WIDTH THAT CANNOT SHOW IT.
                    A text input clips rather than wraps, so below the small
                    breakpoint the field holds a 44-character key the operator
                    can only scroll through — on the card where he is choosing
                    which key to remove. From `sm` up the field shows the whole
                    thing and this echo would be a duplicate, so it is hidden
                    there rather than rendered twice. %>
                <span class="w-full break-all font-mono text-3xs text-muted sm:hidden"
                      x-text="slots[i]"></span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </template>

          <p class="mt-2 text-3xs"
             :class="liveCount < required ? 'text-danger-ink' : 'text-muted'"
             x-text="liveCount + ' of ' + maxSlots + ' slots filled; ' + required + ' signatures are required, so the set cannot go below ' + required"></p>
        </section>

        <section class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3">
          <p class="label-upper text-xs mb-1">
            Step 2 — wallets that will sign (<span x-text="required"></span> required, in order)
          </p>
          <p class="text-3xs text-muted mb-2">
            The first one leads: it fills account 0 and pays the network fee.
            <strong>A wallet that signs cannot be evicted by this transaction</strong>,
            so do not name one you are removing.
          </p>

          <template x-for="(a, i) in authorizers" :key="'a' + i">
            <div class="mb-1.5 rounded-md border border-subtle bg-surface p-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-3xs font-bold uppercase text-muted"
                      x-text="i === 0 ? 'lead' : 'wallet ' + (i + 1)"></span>
                <select name="authorizers[]" x-model="authorizers[i]"
                        class="<%= signer_field_class %>">
                  <option value="">— choose —</option>
                  <%# THE NAME LEADS, THE ADDRESS STAYS. A select option cannot
                      hold an avatar, so the holder is prefixed to the address
                      rather than substituted for it, and the chosen wallet gets
                      the full treatment on the line below. %>
                  <% Array(eligible).each do |address| %>
                    <option value="<%= address %>"><%= identities[address]&.display_name || "unresolved" %> · <%= address %><%= " (this server)" if address == server_address %></option>
                  <% end %>
                </select>
              </div>

              <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 pl-18"
                   x-show="authorizers[i]" x-cloak>
                <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full
                             bg-cover bg-center text-xs font-bold text-white"
                      x-show="identityFor(authorizers[i])" x-cloak
                      :style="avatarStyle(authorizers[i])"
                      x-text="initialsFor(authorizers[i])"></span>
                <span aria-hidden="true"
                      class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full
                             border border-dashed border-strong text-3xs font-bold text-muted"
                      x-show="!identityFor(authorizers[i])" x-cloak>?</span>
                <span class="text-xs"
                      :class="identityFor(authorizers[i]) ? 'font-bold text-heading' : 'text-muted'"
                      x-text="labelFor(authorizers[i])"></span>
                <span class="rounded px-1 py-px text-3xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
                      :class="identityFor(authorizers[i]) ? 'bg-surface-alt' : 'border border-dashed border-strong'"
                      x-text="kindFor(authorizers[i])"></span>
                <span class="w-full break-all font-mono text-3xs text-muted sm:hidden"
                      x-text="authorizers[i]"></span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </template>

          <%# AN ANNOUNCED LIVE REGION, mounted empty and only toggled visible.
              This sentence says the rotation he is assembling would be rejected
              by the chain — "legible and unannounced is only half a fix", and
              it is sharper here than on most error lines because the operator
              is switching wallets and reading a roster while it appears. It is
              `alert`/assertive because it answers an action he is waiting on.
              Mounted-and-hidden rather than `<template x-if>`: a live region
              inserted alongside its own content is not reliably announced. %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-3xs text-danger-ink" x-show="conflict()" x-cloak
             role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true"
             x-text="conflict()"></p>
        </section>

        <div class="pt-1">
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">
            Arm this eviction
          </button>
          <p class="mt-1 text-3xs text-muted">
            Arming records the proposed set and builds nothing. You will review it
            before any wallet is opened.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>

<script>
  // PROPS LIVE IN THE FACTORY, NOT IN THE ATTRIBUTE. An `x-data` expression
  // carrying interpolated server values needs quoting that survives ERB, and a
  // single stray double quote silently kills the whole component — the element
  // renders, nothing binds, and no error appears anywhere. Reading the values
  // off data-* attributes in init() has no quoting hazard at all.
  window.evictionPlanner = function() {
    return {
      slots: [],
      authorizers: [],
      maxSlots: 3,
      required: 2,
      identities: {},

      init: function(el) {
        this.maxSlots = parseInt(el.dataset.maxSlots, 10) || 3;
        this.required = parseInt(el.dataset.required, 10) || 2;

        // A MALFORMED MAP MUST NOT TAKE THE PLANNER WITH IT. Everything the
        // identity map feeds is a reading aid; the slots, the selects and the
        // continuity guard all work without it. So a parse failure degrades to
        // "nobody resolves" — which renders as unresolved, the honest answer —
        // rather than throwing inside init() and leaving an inert form.
        try {
          this.identities = JSON.parse(el.dataset.identities || '{}') || {};
        } catch (e) {
          this.identities = {};
        }

        var current = (el.dataset.currentSigners || '').split(',').filter(function(a) { return !!a; });
        this.slots = current.slice(0, this.maxSlots);
        while (this.slots.length < this.maxSlots) this.slots.push('');

        // Pre-seed the signing wallets with the CURRENT set, which is the only
        // set that can authorize anything. Left for the operator to change —
        // the one he must change is the lead, whenever he is evicting it.
        this.authorizers = current.slice(0, this.required);
        while (this.authorizers.length < this.required) this.authorizers.push('');
      },

      get liveCount() {
        return this.slots.filter(function(s) { return !!(s && s.trim()); }).length;
      },

      // ── WHO HOLDS AN ADDRESS ────────────────────────────────────────────
      //
      // A MISS RETURNS null AND IS RENDERED AS A MISS. The operator types into
      // these fields, so most of what passes through here is a partial address
      // that matches nothing — and a lookup that fell back to a placeholder
      // name would put a person beside a key that is not theirs. On this card
      // that is the one error worth more than all the others: he is choosing
      // which key to REMOVE.
      identityFor: function(address) {
        var key = (address || '').trim();
        if (!key) return null;
        return this.identities[key] || null;
      },

      labelFor: function(address) {
        var id = this.identityFor(address);
        return id ? id.name : 'No linked account';
      },

      kindFor: function(address) {
        var id = this.identityFor(address);
        return id ? id.kind : 'Unresolved';
      },

      // The initials are the FALLBACK, so they are blank whenever a real avatar
      // is painted behind them — one element carries both, and an initial
      // showing through an uploaded photo is what the emptiness prevents.
      initialsFor: function(address) {
        var id = this.identityFor(address);
        return (id && !id.avatar) ? id.initials : '';
      },

      avatarStyle: function(address) {
        var id = this.identityFor(address);
        if (!id) return '';
        if (id.avatar) return 'background-image: url(' + id.avatar + ')';
        return 'background-color: ' + id.color;
      },

      // COMPACT ON EVICT. Clearing in place would leave a gap, which turf-vault
      // refuses (SignerSetTooSmall) — so ordinary use never produces one. The
      // server still refuses a gap if a request arrives with one; this is
      // convenience, not the guard.
      evict: function(i) {
        this.slots.splice(i, 1);
        this.slots.push('');
      },

      // The refusal the CHAIN would give, said here first. Continuity is the
      // one rule an operator reliably trips: he picks the wallets he has, and
      // one of them is the key he is trying to remove.
      conflict: function() {
        var live = this.slots.map(function(s) { return (s || '').trim(); })
                             .filter(function(s) { return !!s; });
        var chosen = this.authorizers.filter(function(a) { return !!a; });
        var lost = chosen.filter(function(a) { return live.indexOf(a) === -1; });
        if (lost.length) {
          return lost.join(', ') + ' would sign this rotation and then be evicted by it. ' +
                 'turf-vault requires ' + this.required + ' of the authorizing wallets to ' +
                 'survive, so this set would be rejected (SignerContinuityRequired).';
        }
        var dupes = chosen.filter(function(a, i) { return chosen.indexOf(a) !== i; });
        if (dupes.length) {
          return dupes.join(', ') + ' is named twice. Every signature must come from a ' +
                 'distinct wallet (DuplicateSigner).';
        }
        return '';
      }
    };
  };
</script>
